Every autumn, senior leaders are asked the same question about pupil premium: did it work? This year the answer has to be published by 31 December, and the DfE expects it to rest on measurable outcomes, not on how staff felt it went.

The trap is that the group you funded in September is rarely the group you measure in July. Pupils join, leave, move onto roll and come off it. A figure can rise because of who left, and fall because of who arrived.

## How pupil premium is awarded

The money follows pupils, not schools. For the 2026–27 financial year (1 April 2026 to 31 March 2027), the rates are:

| Who | Per pupil |
| --- | --- |
| Looked-after and previously looked-after children | £2,690 |
| Free school meals in the last six years (“Ever 6”), reception to Year 6 | £1,550 |
| Free school meals in the last six years (“Ever 6”), Years 7 to 11 | £1,100 |
| Service children (on top of any other rate) | £360 |

Allocations are based on the October census, so this year's money reflects who was on roll in October 2025. A pupil who arrives in January brings no funding until the next census. A pupil who leaves in March has already been paid for.

One change this year matters. From September 2026, every family on Universal Credit can claim free meals, but pupil premium stays with the existing income threshold. More pupils will be eating free school meals than attract pupil premium, so “FSM” and “disadvantaged” are no longer quite the same group in your data.

Source: [DfE conditions of grant 2026 to 2027](https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/pupil-premium-allocations-and-conditions-of-grant-2026-to-2027/pupil-premium-conditions-of-grant-for-the-2026-to-2027-financial-year) and [technical note](https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/pupil-premium-allocations-and-conditions-of-grant-2026-to-2027/pupil-premium-2026-to-2027-technical-note).

## How it's meant to be spent, and proved

The DfE's guidance groups spending into three tiers, following the Education Endowment Foundation:

- **High-quality teaching:** training, mentoring and coaching, recruitment and retention. The guidance says this should be the top priority.
- **Targeted academic support:** tuition, literacy and numeracy interventions, teaching assistants.
- **Wider strategies:** attendance, behaviour and wellbeing support, breakfast clubs, enrichment, work with parents.

Every funded activity has to come from the DfE's menu of approaches. Attendance is on it, and the guidance asks schools to consider it, given how far absence has risen for disadvantaged pupils since the pandemic.

Then comes the evidence. Any school with more than five eligible pupils publishes a strategy statement on the DfE template by 31 December. Part B reports how disadvantaged pupils did last year. The guidance is clear that evaluation should measure outcomes against clear intended targets, not rely on the views of the staff who ran the activity, and should decide what continues and what stops.

Source: [Using pupil premium: guidance for school leaders](https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/pupil-premium/using-pupil-premium-guidance-for-school-leaders), updated August 2026.

## A mentoring programme that seemed to work

Our demo school funded attendance mentoring for disadvantaged pupils in Year 10. By the summer, their attendance was up 2.2 points on last year. That's a line that writes itself into a strategy statement.

But the group had changed. Five pupils had left during the year: a managed move, two families moving away, two places in alternative provision. Last year they averaged 72%. Three pupils joined, averaging 93%.

| Disadvantaged pupils, now in Year 10 | Last year, as Year 9 | This year | Change |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Everyone in the group at the time | 40 pupils, 88.0% | 38 pupils, 90.2% | +2.2 points |
| Pupils here throughout | 35 pupils, 90.3% | 35 pupils, 90.0% | −0.3 points |

*Invented demo school data.*

Count only the 35 pupils who were there both years, and their attendance didn't improve. The headline rise came from who left and who arrived, not from the mentoring.

That doesn't mean the programme failed. It may have stopped a slide, or helped a handful of pupils while others dipped. But it isn't the evidence it first looked like, and a governor or the trust could spot the same thing.

## Three comparisons, three questions

The same 90.2% gives three different answers, depending on what you set it against. None is wrong. Each answers a different question.

| Compare this year's Year 10 (90.2%) with | The question it answers | Demo school |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Last year's Year 10, as it was (89.5%) | Is the school doing better at this stage? | Yes, +0.7 |
| Last year's Year 9, as it was (88.0%) | Has this year group's figure moved? | Yes, +2.2 |
| The same pupils last year (90.3% → 90.0%) | Have these pupils changed? | No, −0.3 |

For a pupil premium review, the third question is usually the one that matters. You spent the money on particular pupils, so the fair test is whether those pupils changed. The first two are still worth reporting, because they're what governors and the trust will compare you on.

## Before you write Part B

Three checks before an improvement goes into your strategy statement:

1. **Who left, and who joined?** If the group changed, look at the pupils who were there throughout as well as the headline.
2. **Compared with when?** Compare the same point last year, counted in school days. Term dates move, and a calendar date won't line up.
3. **What would have happened anyway?** Attendance often dips as pupils move into Key Stage 4. A flat line from Year 9 to Year 10 can be a good result, so check it against last year's Year 10 too.

The matched view has limits too. It leaves out the pupils who joined, so it says nothing about how well you support new arrivals. And it can't include Year 7, who have no last year to compare with. Use it alongside the headline figure, not instead of it.

## What EarlySight does

EarlySight puts all three questions on the same page, so you don't have to rebuild them in a spreadsheet in December.

- The cohort tables switch between the whole year group and “Pupils here throughout”.
- The SLT week-by-week charts show each year as it was, with a switch to today's pupils.
- Every “same point last year” comparison is counted in school days.
- “Who is this falling on” splits each measure by disadvantage and need, so you can see whether a change belongs to the pupils the money was for.
- A governors' summary is written from the same figures, with no pupil named.

A raw number tells you who is in the room. The pupil premium question is whether the pupils you funded are better off.
